":"commune-summorum-pontificum","proper":"Secret"},"text":"Oblatis muneribus, quaesumus, Domine, Ecclesiam tuam benignus illumina: ut et gregis tui proficiat ubique successus, et grati fiant nomini tuo, te gubernante, pastores. Per Dominum nostrum.\n","text_status":null,"translations":[{"caution":"A lay hand missal of the 1945 revision, printed about 1955-56, not an approved liturgical translation and never an altar book. Its Holy Week is the pre-1955 rite, its sanctoral is not the 1962 sanctoral, and its second and third orations are not those of 1962. It is carried only where a 1962 formulary is the same prayer, and nothing here may be used for recitation.\n\nLiteralhistoricalLasanceEnglishfromordinaryStMartinpages. Target and old Feltoe pageimages read200+400dpi; Lasance1287-1288 read150dpi. Human English transcribed from pages, not OCR. No saint is named in this prayer. Target nostrum is a longer conclusion cue than source Per Dominum.; no conclusion text is invented. UnderlyingLatin has finitequalifiedadaptationbasis, separate fromEnglishnonrenewal; noapproved1962Englishclaimed.","lang":"en","rights":"public-domain","source_id":"edition.francis-xavier-lasance.the-new-roman-missal.benziger-revised-1945","source_label":"The New Roman Missal in Latin and English, revised edition, by F. X. Lasance and Francis Augustine Walsh (New York: Benziger Brothers, 1945), ca. 1955-56 printing (Internet Archive delivery locator fr-lasance-missal-1937; locator mislabelled)","text":"In Thy loving kindness, we beseech Thee, O Lord, be moved by the offering of our gifts and enlighten Thy Church: that Thy flock may prosper everywhere and the shepherds, under Thy guidance, may be rendered acceptable to Thee. Through our Lord."}],"unavailable_translations":null,"untranslated":null},{"citations":[{"book":"Matthew","loci":{"hebrew":[{"chapter":16,"first":18,"last":18}],"hebrew-unnumbered-titles":[{"chapter":16,"first":18,"last":18}],"vulgate":[{"chapter":16,"first":18,"last":18}]},"ref